                value = int(v)\n            except (TypeError, ValueError):\n                raise ValueError(f\"must be an integer >= {minimum}, got {v!r}\") from None\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"must be >= {minimum}, got {value}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nError wrapping in __get__ adds \"NAME: \" prefix, so message contains variable name. Empty string int('') raises ValueError -> wrapped with name. Good.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter __init__ with keyword-only `max_message_chars: int | None = None`. Validate: `if max_message_chars is not None: if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32: raise ValueError(...)`.\n\nThen in format: after `super().format(record)` — but super().format calls record.getMessage() and sets record.message. We must not mutate record.msg/args. Slicing: message length budget applied to record.message, prefix not counted include? Instruction: budget only message body, not prefix/time etc. Existing formatting uses `%(message)s` in fmt. Approach: compute `message = record.getMessage()`, then truncate message, and format prefix manually? But formatTime etc.\n\nSimplest safe: temporarily? Can't mutate record.message because shared record. But can compute the formatted output and replace occurrences? Since msg = super().format(record) includes prefix + message. If message occurs in output, we can replace `msg.find(record.message)` position with truncated. But record.message could appear in prefix too (e.g., filename)? Risky though tests probably just check output content contains truncated message and prefix. Better to construct formatted without shared record mutation: We could copy record? `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` creates copy with msg/args copied; formatting copy doesn't affect original. But super().format writes record.message (for copy only) and handles exception formatting (exc_text caching on copy). However, caching exc_text on copy doesn't affect original; overhead fine. Yet tests may check original record unchanged. Copy is safe.\n\nBut is copying appropriate? Alternatively manually manage: create shallow copy via `copy.copy(record)`? copy module allowed? imports allowed list doesn't include copy. But we can use `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` from logging module, no import needed. That constructs LogRecord from dict; msg, args, exc_info carried. Then apply budget to copy's message.\n\nHowever, `super().format(record)` in the original; we need truncated message before prefix formatting. Approach:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n        target = record\n        if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > max_chars:\n                omitted = \"...<omitted>...\"\n                remaining = max_chars - len(omitted)\n                head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n                tail = remaining - head\n                target = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n                target.msg = message[:head] + omitted + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n                target.args = None\n        msg = super().format(target)\n        ...\n```\n\nWait instruction says \"先完成 %s 等参数替换，再计算消息长度\" — record.getMessage() does that. And must not rewrite shared record msg/args. Using copy target: we set target.msg to pre-substituted full message truncated; target.args=None. Then super().format(target) calls getMessage returns msg (no args). Fine. But `record.message` is used for newline prefix: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message`. For target with truncated message, we should use `target.message`? `super().format(target)` sets target.message. Use `getattr(target, \"message\", \"\")`. Actually original code uses record.message; for the copy path we need target.message. Use `target.message` works for both since super().format sets record.message in normal path too. But if keep_full_message True, no truncation, target=record; super sets record.message as before. Fine.\n\nEdge: exception info is included in msg by super().format as \"Traceback ...\" appended after message. If we truncate message before, exception traceback remains full. Good.\n\nEdge: budget exceeding but also newline handling. Fine.\n\nCareful: logging.makeLogRecord copies record.__dict__ — includes message perhaps from prior formatting; super().format recalculates getMessage -> uses msg/args. We set msg/args. Good. It also copies exc_text if cached; for same record maybe None. Fine.\n\nPotential issue `logging.makeLogRecord` triggers `created` etc fine.\n\nDefault init: in `_setup_logger`, read `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Import `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Is there circular import risk? environ imports no logger. Fine. logger currently imports os etc. Add import.\n\nThen `NewLineFormatter(_FORMAT, datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT, use_color=..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS)`.\n\nRepeated init: `_setup_logger` creates new formatter each call, applies current config. Good. But cache: envs descriptor caches. Good.\n\n3. stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNeed to see exact full logger.py lines 68-107 to make replace_text unique. Let me read."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9554,"completion_tokens":1626,"total_tokens":11180,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1526},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5458},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}